-> T26 Phase 3b: `if <var> ∈/∉ TypeName` on an unresolved variable decides
-> from its domain instead of forcing resolution, and — when genuinely
-> mixed — runs the block with `<var>` shadowed to the narrowed domain.
-> Block-scoped only, same rule as `loop <var> { }`: the outer variable is
-> never touched by this.
Status = { "Success" } ∪ { tag = "Error", code ∈ Number }

-> Mixed case: entering the Object branch narrows s down to just the
-> Schema alternative, so a matching object resolves cleanly inside it.
s ∈ Status
if s ∈ Object {
    s = { tag = "Error", code = 500 }
    assert s.code = 500
}

-> Negation: entering the ∉ Object branch narrows down to just the
-> {"Success"} alternative — a single-element domain, so it's already
-> resolved the moment the block is entered, no `=` needed.
u ∈ Status
if u ∉ Object {
    assert u = "Success"
}

-> Already-decided cases don't need narrowing at all.
v ∈ { "a", "b", "c" }
if v ∈ String {
    assert true
}
if v ∈ Number {
    assert false -> unreachable: this branch must never run
}

-> The outer variable is never resolved by entering a narrowed branch —
-> only the block-scoped shadow is. If the Object-branch narrowing above
-> had leaked into the outer w, resolving it to the *other* (String)
-> alternative afterward would now be impossible. It isn't.
w ∈ Status
if w ∈ Object {
    w = { tag = "Error", code = 1 }
}
w = "Success"
assert w = "Success"
